    "explanation": "🔑 Signal: \"have made many drivers <b>___</b> to electric cars\" — the causative verb <b>make</b> + object + verb.<br>Rule: after <b>make somebody</b> (active voice, causative meaning \"cause/force someone to do something\"), the following verb must be the <b>bare infinitive</b> (no \"to\"), e.g. \"make him laugh\", \"make drivers <strong>switch</strong>\". This is a fixed pattern — unlike verbs such as \"want/expect/allow\" which take a to-infinitive after the object, \"make\" (and \"let\", \"help\") drop the \"to\".<br>✗ <b>switching</b> — a gerund/-ing form; \"make\" is not followed by an -ing form after its object (that pattern belongs to verbs like \"keep somebody doing\").<br>✗ <b>to switch</b> — a full to-infinitive; this is the pattern for \"want/ask/expect + object + to-infinitive\", not for the causative \"make\" in the active voice.<br>✗ <b>switched</b> — a past form/past participle; it cannot follow \"make + object\" as the main describing verb and would wrongly suggest a passive or completed meaning.<br>✓ <b>switch</b> is correct: the bare infinitive completes the \"make + object + bare infinitive\" structure."
