    "explanation": "🔑 Signal: \"make corals <b>___</b> the colorful algae\" — the causative verb <b>make</b> + object + verb.<br>Rule: after <b>make somebody/something</b> (active voice, causative meaning \"cause/force to do something\"), the following verb must be the <b>bare infinitive</b> (no \"to\"): \"make him laugh\", \"make corals <strong>lose</strong> their algae\". This is a fixed pattern — unlike \"want/expect/allow\", which take object + to-infinitive, \"make\" (and \"let\", \"help\") drop the \"to\".<br>✗ losing — a gerund/-ing form; \"make + object\" is never followed by -ing (that pattern belongs to verbs like \"keep something doing\").<br>✗ to lose — a full to-infinitive; this fits \"cause corals to lose\" or \"force corals to lose\", but never the causative \"make\" in the active voice.<br>✗ lost — a past form/past participle; it cannot follow \"make + object\" as the action verb and would wrongly suggest a passive or completed meaning.<br>✓ <b>lose</b> is the bare infinitive that correctly completes \"make + object + bare infinitive\"."
