    "type": "tf",
    "section": "Reading",
    "passage": "I spend most of my holidays walking along coastlines, not sailing, so you know I love the sea best with solid ground under my feet. Old towers, however, pull me like magnets, and last spring I finally visited the one I had read about for years: Hook Lighthouse on the south-east coast of Ireland, a 36-meter (118-foot) tower that has guided ships for about 800 years — one of the oldest working lighthouses in the world. There is a car park near the tower, but I don't drive, so I took a bus to the tip of the peninsula and joined a guided tour.<br><br>Our tour began at the heavy black door at the bottom of the tower. We climbed 115 stone steps inside walls up to four meters thick, stopping at the old keepers' rooms, where our guide told us stories about the monks who kept a warning fire burning on this headland centuries before the tower was built. Standing at last on the balcony, high above the waves crashing on the black rocks below, I felt both nervous and amazed.<br><br>We had started at 11 a.m., and after our slow, story-filled climb we reached the balcony at around 1 p.m. We stayed there for a long while, watching seabirds circle over the endless grey-green sea. The view was unforgettable.",
