    "explanation": "🔑 Signal: \"<b>Despite</b> + noun phrase\" must be converted into \"<b>Although</b> + subject + verb (clause)\".<br><b>Rule:</b> Despite/In spite of are prepositions and are followed by a <b>noun / noun phrase / V-ing</b> (here: \"the popularity of the singer\"), while Although/Though/Even though are conjunctions and must be followed by a <b>full clause</b> (subject + verb). So the noun \"popularity\" must be turned back into the adjective it came from — <b>popular</b> — and given its own subject and verb: \"the singer <b>was popular</b>\".<br>Derivation: popularity (noun) → popular (adjective); \"the popularity of the singer\" → \"the singer was popular\". The second clause \"few people bought tickets to her concert\" is simply carried over unchanged, joined by a comma since \"Although\" now opens the sentence.<br>Model answer: Although the singer <strong>was popular, few people bought tickets to her concert.</strong><br>✗ \"Although the singer popularity...\" — keeps the noun form after Although; a conjunction needs a verb (was), not a bare noun phrase, so this is not a grammatical clause.<br>✗ \"Although the singer's popularity, few people bought...\" — still a noun phrase (no verb), so it fails the same clause requirement; Although can never be directly followed by a possessive noun phrase.<br>✗ Omitting the comma before \"few\" — when the Although-clause comes first, a comma is required to separate it from the main clause, matching the original two-part meaning (singer was popular / but ticket sales were poor)."
