    "passage": "I travel a lot for work, mostly by plane, not by train, so you know I'm used to airports, security queues and long waits, but my golden rule of travel is never to waste a whole day just getting somewhere. Last month I discovered the journey of my dreams: the high-speed train from Tokyo to Osaka on Japan's famous Shinkansen line. There are many ways to travel between the two cities, but the Shinkansen leaves from the city centre, so I simply walked to the station from my hotel. I was travelling with a Japanese colleague.<br><br>When we reached the platform, the train arrived exactly on time, to the second. For the next two and a half hours, instead of squeezing into a narrow plane seat, we relaxed in wide, quiet seats, watched the countryside rush past at over 280 kilometres per hour, and even saw Mount Fuji through the window. Besides the comfortable seats, the train had a food trolley selling hot meals, screens showing travel information, and staff who bowed politely as they entered each carriage. Occasionally, my colleague explained the sights we were passing. I was both surprised and delighted.<br><br>We left Tokyo at 9 a.m., arrived in Osaka just before noon, and still had the whole afternoon free for our meetings. The trip was unforgettable."
