    "passage": "Street food is an important part of daily life in many Asian cities, and Ho Chi Minh City is no exception. Every evening, small stalls appear on busy corners, selling dishes such as banh mi, grilled corn, and fresh spring rolls.<br><br>Street food is popular for several reasons. First, it is cheap, so students and workers can enjoy a tasty meal without spending much money. Second, it is quick: most dishes are cooked right in front of the customers and served within minutes. Street food also brings people together. Friends and families often sit on small plastic stools, share dishes, and chat after a long day. For visitors from other countries, tasting street food is one of the best ways to learn about local culture. Many sellers have prepared the same dish for decades, and their recipes are passed down from parents to children. However, customers should choose stalls that look clean and busy, because freshly cooked food is usually safer.<br><br>In short, street food is more than just a quick meal. It reflects the history, flavors, and friendly spirit of the city, and it deserves to be protected as part of local culture.",
